# [[ .Name ]]

A [tug](https://github.com/cuonggt/tug) app: Go handlers render React pages
through Inertia, with no API in between. People register and verify their
email, log in, with a code from an authenticator app too if they turn that
on, reset a forgotten password by email, and change their profile,
password and appearance in their settings.

```sh
tug dev      # run it on http://127.0.0.1:8080, rebuilt and reloaded as it changes
tug gen      # write the TypeScript of the pages' props and the routes
tug build    # build it into one binary, frontend included
go test ./...
```

- `main.go`: the server, its pages and routes, and the health check at
  `/up`. A page is declared with `tug.Page[Props]("Name")`, and its
  component is `resources/js/pages/Name.tsx`.
- `auth.go`: registering, logging in and out, a forgotten password, and
  the page that asks for the password again, on tug's package `auth`. A
  route for users is wrapped in `usersOnly`, whose handler is handed the
  user, and in `verified` or `passwordConfirmed` for what needs them.
- `verify.go`: verifying an email by a mailed link. `twofactor.go`:
  two-factor logins. `settings.go`: the settings pages. `mail.go`: the
  mail, in plain text and HTML.
- `users.go`: the users, in SQLite (`app.db`, or `DB_PATH`), through
  `database/sql`, and the migrations that make and change its tables.
- `jobs.go`: the background jobs, in the same database, for tug's package
  `queue`, which runs them beside the server. The mail goes by jobs, so a
  mail server that's down, or a restart, doesn't lose any: a job that
  fails runs again later. Jobs can run on a schedule too, as `prune-jobs`
  does every night. `QUEUE_WORKERS` is how many run at once.
- `resources/js`: React with Tailwind and [shadcn/ui](https://ui.shadcn.com)
  components, which `npx shadcn@latest add` adds more of. The layouts are
  in `layouts/`, and `app.tsx` says which page has which.
- `resources/js/tug`: the TypeScript tug gen writes from the Go types. It
  isn't edited by hand; tug dev and tug build write it again.
- `.env`: read by tug dev. `APP_KEY` encrypts the sessions and two-factor
  secrets and signs the links in mail, and a deployed app needs one of its
  own, set in its environment, with `APP_URL`, its own address, and
  `MAIL_HOST` and the rest of `.env.example` for its mail. In development,
  mail is written out where tug dev shows it, links and all.
- `Dockerfile`: the app as one static binary on a distroless image, with
  the database on a volume.
